## How it works

```
┌────────┐   audio   ┌──────────┐   text   ┌──────────┐   polish   ┌────────┐
│  Caps  │ ────────▶ │ Recorder │ ───────▶ │ Streaming│ ─────────▶ │ Polish │
│  Lock  │           │  (Rust)  │          │   STT    │            │  LLM   │
└────────┘           └──────────┘          └──────────┘            └────┬───┘
                                                                        │
                          ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                          ▼
                     ┌──────────┐   paste   ┌─────────┐
                     │  Paster  │ ────────▶ │ Cursor  │
                     │  (Rust)  │           │ in app  │
                     └──────────┘           └─────────┘
                          ▲
                          │
                     ┌──────────┐
                     │ Learning │  watches edits, infers rules,
                     │ engine   │  applies them on the next take
                     └──────────┘
```

The hot path — hotkey → record → STT → polish → paste — is pure Rust. No JS runtime in the way of latency.

| Crate / dir | Role |
|---|---|
| [crates/core](crates/core) | STT client and shared types |
| [crates/recorder](crates/recorder) | CoreAudio capture, ring buffer, VAD |
| [crates/hotkey](crates/hotkey) | Caps Lock / global hotkey via CGEventTap |
| [crates/paster](crates/paster) | Accessibility-driven cursor paste |
| [crates/voice-polish](crates/voice-polish) | LLM polish + the learning loop |
| [crates/backend](crates/backend) | Local IPC backend the desktop app talks to |
| [crates/control-plane](crates/control-plane) | Optional: shared infra on Fly.io |
| [desktop](desktop) | Tauri 2 + React 19 dashboard |
| [landing](landing) | Next.js marketing site |

---

## The learning loop

Most dictation tools ship a *dictionary* — a static list you maintain by hand. Said ships a *learning loop*.

It watches what you correct in your editor (Anish → Aneesh, react → React, *kar do* → कर दो), infers the rule, weights it by how often the same correction repeats, and **promotes it from a weak signal to a hard rule** once it's confirmed enough times. The rule then applies on every future dictation, automatically.

You never manage a list. You talk and edit, and Said quietly gets better at sounding like you.

The full design is in [docs/permission-learning.md](docs/permission-learning.md). The code lives in [crates/voice-polish/src/learning.rs](crates/voice-polish/src/learning.rs).

---

## Privacy

- Audio is captured locally and streamed only to the STT/LLM endpoints whose keys you provided.
- Your keys, OAuth tokens, and learned rules sit in your macOS Keychain and a local SQLite DB at `~/VoicePolish/`.
- There is no Said cloud. No telemetry. No crash reporter. No shared database.
- Screen-context awareness is opt-in and never persisted — window text is read at dictation time, sent with the polish request, and dropped.

---

## Alternatives

If Said doesn't fit, here's an honest map of the space:

| | **Said** | Wispr Flow | SuperWhisper | MacWhisper | Apple Dictation |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Hinglish / code-switching | ✅ | ⚠️ | ⚠️ | ⚠️ | ❌ |
| Hindi (Devanagari) | ✅ | ⚠️ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Auto-polish / LLM rewrite | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ⚠️ | ❌ |
| Learns from your edits | ✅ | Manual | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |
| Auto-paste at cursor | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ⚠️ | ✅ |
| Open source | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | — |
| Self-host / BYO keys | ✅ | ❌ | Partial | ✅ | — |
| Pricing | Free | Subscription | One-time | One-time | Free |

Said is the right pick if you want to dictate in Hinglish, you want full control of where your audio goes, or you'd rather run open code than rent a feature. If you're English-only and don't mind a subscription, the alternatives are excellent — pick what fits your workflow.

